---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="End-to-End Contract Test for a Multi-Service AI System · San Lee"
  description="One labeled note traced through an API, classifier, writeback, and knowledge agent to verify real schemas and expose a consumer contract that drops a field."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/the-system-run.html"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* One-note run plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped companion to the
       system diagram: a single note through notes-api, classifier, writeback,
       and kb-agent. Max-width 260 keeps viewBox units 1:1 with CSS px on every
       gate width (same measured discipline as .jtbd-strip / .lab-topo). */
    .run-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Dropped field: dashed oxide — dual-encoded by dash + oxide + label, not
       colour alone. The consumer never mapped region (prose step 3). */
    .run-plate .rn-drop {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 3 2.5;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-drop-box {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 3 2.5;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-drop-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .run-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .run-plate marker path.rn-head-drop { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .run-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="the-system.html">&larr; Back to The System</a>
    <h1>One Note, End to End</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">One gold note through the real schemas. Writeback maps two labels and drops region.</p>
    <p class="ex-caption">
      The article text and all three predicted labels are real: gold row
      <code>g002</code> from the classifier's eval set, a prediction that matches
      both the hand label and the LLM judge on every axis. The note's title,
      <code>id</code>, and timestamps are illustrative placeholders.
    </p>

    <!-- Run companion to the-system diagram: one note through the four seams.
         region is the load-bearing asymmetry (dashed oxide drop off classifier).
         Ordered list below still carries the five steps and real payloads.
         ADR-010 N/A — no interactive nodes. -->
    <svg class="run-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 118" role="img"
         aria-label="One note end to end: notes-api creates the note, the classifier returns three labels, writeback keeps two as tags and drops region unmapped, then kb-agent can search by tag.">
      <title>One note end to end: notes-api creates the note, the classifier returns three labels, writeback keeps two as tags and drops region unmapped, then kb-agent can search by tag.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="rn-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="rn-arrow-drop" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path class="rn-head-drop" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Four stage boxes L→R. rx matches --radius-sm (2px). -->
      <rect class="rn-box" x="2" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="rn-title" x="30" y="26" text-anchor="middle">notes-api</text>
      <text class="rn-micro" x="30" y="38" text-anchor="middle">POST</text>

      <rect class="rn-box" x="68" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="rn-title" x="96" y="26" text-anchor="middle">classifier</text>
      <text class="rn-micro" x="96" y="38" text-anchor="middle">3 fields</text>

      <rect class="rn-box" x="134" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="rn-title" x="162" y="26" text-anchor="middle">writeback</text>
      <text class="rn-micro" x="162" y="38" text-anchor="middle">2 tags</text>

      <rect class="rn-box" x="200" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="rn-title" x="228" y="26" text-anchor="middle">kb-agent</text>
      <text class="rn-micro" x="228" y="38" text-anchor="middle">search</text>

      <!-- Three L→R muted edges — open pipeline through the run. -->
      <path class="rn-edge" d="M 58 28 L 66 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="rn-edge" d="M 124 28 L 132 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="rn-edge" d="M 190 28 L 198 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

      <!-- Dropped region: branches down from classifier, never reaches writeback.
           Dashed oxide + label dual-encode the unmapped field. -->
      <path class="rn-drop" d="M 96 50 L 96 72" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow-drop)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="rn-drop-box" x="58" y="76" width="76" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="rn-drop-text" x="96" y="91" text-anchor="middle">region dropped</text>

      <text class="rn-micro" x="162" y="70" text-anchor="middle">cat + domain only</text>
      <text class="rn-micro" x="130" y="112" text-anchor="middle">same note, real schemas</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="run-plate-caption">
      Create, classify, write two tags, search. The dashed branch is the field
      the consumer never mapped &mdash; step&nbsp;3 below names it.
    </p>

    <ol class="example-run">
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">A note is created</p>
        <p class="ex-note"><code>POST /notes</code> takes a title and content. It
          returns <code>201</code> immediately and schedules the enrichment as a
          background task, so <code>tags</code> is still empty at this point.</p>
        <pre><code>POST /notes
&#123;
  "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
  "content": "F/A-18E Hornets assigned to the \"Eagles\" of Strike Fighter Attack Squadron 115 ... in support of Operation Iraqi Freedom."
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">The classifier is forced to answer in schema</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The background task POSTs the text to the classifier's
          <code>/classify</code> endpoint. One <code>claude-sonnet-5</code> call
          with <code>tool_choice</code> pinned to the <code>classify_article</code>
          tool forces the model to answer only as an enum-validated tool call,
          which the endpoint returns as
          <code>&#123;category, operational_domain, region&#125;</code>. No confidence
          score, by design.</p>
        <pre><code>// forced tool_use emitted by the model
&#123;
  "name": "classify_article",
  "input": &#123;
    "category": "operations",
    "operational_domain": "air",
    "region": "middle-east"
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">Labels become namespaced tags</p>
        <p class="ex-note">notes-api owns the writeback. It maps the fields it
          knows to prefixed tags (note the <code>operational_domain</code>
          &rarr; <code>domain:</code> rename) and applies them with replace
          semantics, so reprocessing converges instead of piling up duplicates.
          The user's own tags are preserved. The background task writes through
          the ORM in-process rather than calling the service's own HTTP
          endpoint; the equivalent <code>PUT /notes/&#123;id&#125;/tags</code> exists for
          external callers and carries the same replace semantics.</p>
        <p class="ex-note"><strong>Note what happens to <code>region</code>
          here: nothing.</strong> The classifier grew a third field in v3;
          this consumer still reads two and drops the rest on the floor. It
          doesn't break, because the writeback pulls fields out by name instead
          of assuming the response shape. Wiring <code>region:</code> through is
          pending work, and until it lands this page shows two tags because two
          tags is what actually gets written.</p>
        <p class="ex-note">Tolerating an unknown field is consumer hygiene, not
          a guard. The frozen contract for this seam requires a provider change
          to land with a coordinated consumer update. When the classifier
          shipped <code>region</code>, that update did not happen and no build
          went red &mdash; each repo's &ldquo;contract test&rdquo; asserts
          against its own copy of the shape rather than a shared one. Nothing
          broke here, and nothing would have noticed if it had. The
          <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-004-classify-http-contract.md">SYS-004 amendment</a>
          records it.</p>
        <pre><code>classifier_tags(&#123;
    "category": "operations",
    "operational_domain": "air",
    "region": "middle-east",   # not mapped yet — ignored, not an error
&#125;)
# -&gt; ["category:operations", "domain:air"]

# written in-process; note.tags = merge_tags(note.tags, new_tags)
&#123; "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air"] &#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">The note is now enriched</p>
        <p class="ex-note">Reading it back shows the labels in its flat
          <code>tags</code> list. There is no <code>status</code> field anywhere:
          "enriched" is simply the presence of <code>category:</code> /
          <code>domain:</code> tags, which keeps the schema small and the state
          honest.</p>
        <pre><code>GET /notes/1
&#123;
  "id": 1,
  "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
  "content": "F/A-18E Hornets ... Operation Iraqi Freedom.",
  "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air"],
  "created_at": "...",
  "updated_at": "..."
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">The agent can find it</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The kb-agent's <code>search_notes</code> tool filters by
          tag over the same HTTP seam, so the freshly-tagged note is now retrievable
          for grounding. The result follows the system-wide observation contract
          (SYS-003): a status, a summary, the payload, and a cited source.</p>
        <pre><code>search_notes(tag="domain:air")  -&gt;  GET /notes?tag=domain:air
&#123;
  "status": "success",
  "summary": "1 matching note(s).",
  "payload": [
    &#123;
      "id": 1,
      "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
      "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air"]
    &#125;
  ],
  "source": "notes-api service, http://127.0.0.1:8000/notes"
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>

  </main>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
